MVP Proposal: CRYSTALCORE.OS – Scheduled Delay-Tolerant Routing Engine

---

1. Executive Summary

CRYSTALCORE.OS is a reference implementation of the mathematical framework for scheduled delay‑tolerant networking described in “Mathematical Foundations of Scheduled Delay‑Tolerant Networking”. The project aims to deliver a minimum viable product (MVP) that brings the formal model to life: a deterministic routing engine that computes the earliest possible delivery path for a data bundle through a network of pre‑scheduled contact windows, using the time‑expanded graph (TEG) and Dijkstra’s algorithm proved optimal in the paper.

The MVP will serve as:

· a golden benchmark for heuristic DTN routing algorithms (CGR, SABR),
· an educational tool for understanding the core mathematics,
· a foundation for future expansion into multi‑bundle scheduling, capacity‑aware routing, and interplanetary network simulation.

The deliverable includes a Python library, a command‑line interface, example contact plans, and full documentation, all hosted in a public GitHub repository.

---

2. Background & Motivation

Interplanetary and deep‑space networks cannot rely on the TCP/IP handshake model due to extreme propagation delays (minutes to hours) and intermittent connectivity. The theoretical work establishes a rigorous physical‑layer‑aware model:

· Contacts are defined by start/end times, capacity functions derived from the Shannon–Hartley theorem and free‑space path loss, and constant light‑time delays.
· Bundles carry creation timestamps and lifetimes.
· Routing reduces to a shortest‑path problem on a Time‑Expanded Graph (TEG) constructed from the contact plan.
· The TEG construction is provably correct and optimal for single‑bundle earliest delivery.

Real‑world DTN protocols (BPv7, CCSDS SABR, CGR) implement heuristics that approximate this optimum. An exact, open‑source reference implementation is currently lacking. CRYSTALCORE.OS fills this gap, providing a transparent, testable, and extensible core that aligns directly with the mathematics.

