3. MVP Objectives

1. Core Routing Engine
      Implement the full TEG construction algorithm (Appendix B) and Dijkstra shortest‑path search, exactly as proved in the paper, for a single bundle and a given contact plan.
2. Data Model
      Provide Python classes for:
   · Nodes, Contacts (with piecewise constant capacity), Bundles.
   · Time‑Expanded Graph vertices, waiting edges, and transmission edges.
   · Path representation.
3. I/O and Validation
   · Read contact plans and bundles from a well‑defined JSON/YAML format.
   · Validate that input obeys the model assumptions (e.g., contacts do not overlap for same node pair? Not required, but consistency checks).
   · Output the computed path as a timeline of hops with start/finish times.
4. Correctness Assurance
      Include unit tests that verify:
   · Exact matches with hand‑calculated simple cases.
   · Consistency with the lemmas (e.g., any feasible continuous path maps to a TEG path).
   · Edge cases (bundle too large for any contact, lifetime expiration, multiple possible paths).
5. Documentation & Examples
   · README with quickstart.
   · Jupyter notebook demonstrating a Mars‑Earth relay scenario.
   · API reference.
6. GitHub Repository
      Set up the project with continuous integration (GitHub Actions) for testing and linting, and open the repository for community contributions under a permissive license (MIT).

Out of scope for MVP: Multi‑bundle sequential capacity updating, dynamic contact plan updates, real‑time data streams, GUI, integration with actual DTN stacks (ION, HDTN). These are planned as post‑MVP enhancements.

