4. Technical Approach

4.1 Architecture

The MVP will be a Python 3.10+ library (crystalcore) with a thin CLI wrapper.
Dependencies: numpy (for numerical integration if needed), networkx (graph representation and Dijkstra), pydantic (data validation). All of these are well‑supported and lightweight.

The TEG construction will not pre‑compute the full graph eagerly. Instead, it will generate vertices and edges on‑demand during the Dijkstra search to keep memory usage bounded when dealing with large contact plans. This matches the real‑time CGR philosophy while still guaranteeing optimality.

4.2 Data Flow

```
Contact Plan (JSON) + Bundle (JSON) 
       → Python objects 
       → TEG Builder (creates source vertex, explores outgoing edges) 
       → Dijkstra (with lazy edge generation) 
       → Earliest arrival time & path
       → Output (console, JSON, plot)
```

4.3 Key Algorithms

· Event discretisation: Extract all contact start/end times, add bundle creation time.
· Contact volume integral: Evaluate piecewise constant capacity to decide transmission feasibility.
· Transmission edge creation: For a given departure event t_k and contact κ, solve for finish time and target vertex using the exact formula (B.2). Insert new vertex if needed.
· Dijkstra: Standard min‑priority queue over (arrival_time, vertex). Terminate when any destination vertex is settled and has time ≤ lifetime.

